# Gradle
#
# Every one of these is ANCHORED with a leading slash on purpose. An unanchored
# `build/` matches a directory called `build` at ANY depth — including the Java
# package directory buildSrc/src/main/kotlin/com/heimdall/build/, whose sources
# were silently swallowed and never committed, leaving main unbuildable from a
# fresh clone while `git status` reported a clean tree. `out` and `bin` are just
# as plausible as package names, so they are anchored too.
#
# Add a deeper level here if a module is ever nested more than two directories
# down, rather than removing the anchors.
/.gradle/
/*/.gradle/
/build/
/*/build/
/*/*/build/
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

# IDE
.idea/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.vscode/
.settings/
.classpath
.project
/out/
/*/out/
/*/*/out/
/bin/
/*/bin/
/*/*/bin/

# Boot-smoke scratch: per-row plugin mounts and captured server logs. Anchored to
# the one directory that produces it, for the same reason as the Gradle entries
# above — an unanchored `.work/` would be a trap for whatever needs that name next.
#
# Two entries, one per scenario, rather than a `/smoke/.work*` glob: the glob would
# also swallow a future `smoke/.workflows` or similar, which is exactly the class of
# accident the anchoring above exists to prevent. The connected scenario's directory
# additionally ends up holding whole server data directories — Paper writes its
# .paper-remapped index and bStats into the same mount — so it is not merely logs.
/smoke/.work/
/smoke/.work-connected/

# Local dev stack: compose project with a staged plugin jar, server data
# directories, and a machine-local .env (MC/proxy versions, host ports).
# Anchored like everything else above.
/dev-server/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
